WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.161
This is the executive
briefing for Book 11 of the

2
00:00:03.161 --> 00:00:07.050
Federal Modernization Workbook,
Zero Trust and Assessment.

3
00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:12.147
It runs about thirty minutes, and it's
built for the people who make the calls: the

4
00:00:12.147 --> 00:00:17.400
CISO, the authorizing officials, the CIO,
and the Zero Trust implementation lead.

5
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.212
The book's argument fits
in three sentences.

6
00:00:20.212 --> 00:00:25.812
Zero Trust maturity is a property of the
whole agency, not of any product or tenant.

7
00:00:25.812 --> 00:00:29.951
The federal documents set specific
actions and configurations,

8
00:00:29.951 --> 00:00:34.474
but none of them defines a Zero Trust
score or requires a maturity stage.

9
00:00:34.474 --> 00:00:37.316
And the assessment tools
produce evidence for

10
00:00:37.316 --> 00:00:40.012
named people to act on,
not verdicts to report.

11
00:00:40.012 --> 00:00:44.534
I'll take four questions in order: why
Zero Trust belongs to the whole agency,

12
00:00:44.534 --> 00:00:48.763
what the mandates and the tools
actually require, who owns the posture,

13
00:00:48.763 --> 00:00:53.138
and how to turn findings into a program
the authorizing official can see.

14
00:00:53.138 --> 00:00:58.040
Every decision slide names the decision,
its owner, and when it's needed.

15
00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:01.290
Start with the usual assessment calendar.

16
00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:04.815
The network team reviews the
boundary, keyed by address.

17
00:01:04.815 --> 00:01:09.815
The cloud team runs each provider's
posture scan, keyed by resource tags.

18
00:01:09.815 --> 00:01:13.021
The virtualization team
works a hypervisor checklist,

19
00:01:13.021 --> 00:01:16.227
keyed by whatever the management
plane calls a machine.

20
00:01:16.227 --> 00:01:19.960
At year end someone puts the
three reports behind one cover

21
00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.616
page and calls the result the
agency's Zero Trust maturity.

22
00:01:23.616 --> 00:01:26.352
It's three partial
assessments with one title.

23
00:01:26.352 --> 00:01:29.653
The failure is architectural,
not organizational.

24
00:01:29.653 --> 00:01:33.564
A policy decision evaluates a
subject, a device and a resource,

25
00:01:33.564 --> 00:01:38.778
and it can only do that consistently if
every platform describes them the same way.

26
00:01:38.778 --> 00:01:44.157
A database server that runs as a virtual
machine, sits on an agency subnet and backs

27
00:01:44.157 --> 00:01:49.378
up to a cloud bucket can appear as three
unrelated objects, scored by three tools.

28
00:01:49.378 --> 00:01:52.778
A total across them counts
objects, not architecture.

29
00:01:52.778 --> 00:01:56.291
The federal guidance describes
the enterprise, not a tenant.

30
00:01:56.291 --> 00:02:00.446
Executive Order 14028 defines
zero trust architecture

31
00:02:00.446 --> 00:02:04.690
as working throughout all
aspects of the infrastructure.

32
00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:08.676
M-22-09 asks for enterprise-managed
identities and a

33
00:02:08.676 --> 00:02:12.440
complete inventory of every
device the government operates.

34
00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:17.453
CISA's maturity model asks for agency-wide
buy-in for a common architecture.

35
00:02:17.453 --> 00:02:24.678
And NIST SP 800-207 grants no implicit
trust from network location or ownership.

36
00:02:24.678 --> 00:02:29.403
A scan can confirm that an architecture
is unified; it can't make it unified.

37
00:02:29.403 --> 00:02:34.489
So the first decision: reconcile the
network, every cloud and the virtualization

38
00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:39.652
estate to one authoritative asset and
identity record before scoring any of them.

39
00:02:39.652 --> 00:02:43.349
The book gives that to the owner
of the naming and identity plane,

40
00:02:43.349 --> 00:02:44.814
as a step for this quarter.

41
00:02:44.814 --> 00:02:47.430
Until it's done, report any whole-agency

42
00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:50.810
figure as partial
assessments, not one score.

43
00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:54.185
Two documented incidents
show why this is a

44
00:02:54.185 --> 00:02:57.248
security property and not
a reporting preference.

45
00:02:57.248 --> 00:03:01.712
In May and June 2023, an actor
used a Microsoft consumer signing

46
00:03:01.712 --> 00:03:06.435
key to forge authentication tokens
and read Exchange Online mail.

47
00:03:06.435 --> 00:03:11.789
The federal Cyber Safety Review Board
counted 22 enterprise organizations and 503

48
00:03:11.789 --> 00:03:15.461
related personal accounts,
with access to some mailboxes

49
00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:19.285
for at least six weeks, and
called the intrusion preventable.

50
00:03:19.285 --> 00:03:22.510
The tokens were valid to every
system that checked them.

51
00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:25.189
What caught it was a
custom alert one federal

52
00:03:25.189 --> 00:03:28.085
department had built on
mailbox logs it paid for.

53
00:03:28.085 --> 00:03:31.935
The agency's own evidence found what
the provider's platform didn't.

54
00:03:31.935 --> 00:03:33.997
The second sits beneath the guest.

55
00:03:33.997 --> 00:03:40.075
In February 2023, the ESXiArgs campaign
compromised more than 3,800 VMware ESXi

56
00:03:40.075 --> 00:03:46.522
servers by encrypting the configuration
files of the virtual machines on each host.

57
00:03:46.522 --> 00:03:53.577
In 2024, CVE-2024-37085 gave full hypervisor
control to members of a directory

58
00:03:53.577 --> 00:04:00.410
group named ESX Admins, matched by name
rather than by security identifier.

59
00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:03.463
Microsoft noted that many
security products have

60
00:04:03.463 --> 00:04:06.298
limited visibility into
the hypervisor at all.

61
00:04:06.298 --> 00:04:09.247
Both cases make the same
point from opposite ends.

62
00:04:09.247 --> 00:04:11.497
The most consequential failures happened

63
00:04:11.497 --> 00:04:13.747
where no single platform's
tool was looking.

64
00:04:13.747 --> 00:04:15.834
And there's a consequence for the fix.

65
00:04:15.834 --> 00:04:20.103
Once one plane names and identifies
everything the agency runs,

66
00:04:20.103 --> 00:04:22.760
it's a complete map of the attack surface.

67
00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:26.166
NIST already calls the policy
engine and administrator

68
00:04:26.166 --> 00:04:28.848
the key components of
the entire enterprise.

69
00:04:28.848 --> 00:04:33.910
So the decision: govern the unified plane
as a Tier 0 asset from the day it exists,

70
00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:38.505
with eligible, time-boxed administration,
its own monitored telemetry,

71
00:04:38.505 --> 00:04:42.010
and a design that assumes it
will be attacked directly.

72
00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:46.600
Identity engineering and
the DNS and DDI team own it.

73
00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:50.567
Chapter 02 explains why
programs stall at the

74
00:04:50.567 --> 00:04:54.038
maturity model's Initial
stage, or low in Advanced.

75
00:04:54.038 --> 00:04:59.103
They read CISA's Zero Trust Maturity
Model as a five-pillar shopping list:

76
00:04:59.103 --> 00:05:03.587
identity, devices, networks,
applications and workloads, and data.

77
00:05:03.587 --> 00:05:08.154
But the model runs those pillars through
three cross-cutting capabilities:

78
00:05:08.154 --> 00:05:12.576
visibility and analytics, automation
and orchestration, and governance.

79
00:05:12.576 --> 00:05:17.026
Governance is the one programs fund
least, and the model defines it

80
00:05:17.026 --> 00:05:21.800
as the definition and enforcement of
policy, within and across pillars.

81
00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:26.600
Traditional reads like a failure list:
siloed pillars of policy enforcement,

82
00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:29.525
and limited correlation
of logs and telemetry.

83
00:05:29.525 --> 00:05:32.242
Advanced calls for
cross-pillar coordination

84
00:05:32.242 --> 00:05:35.238
and policy enforcement
integrated across pillars.

85
00:05:35.238 --> 00:05:37.663
No single pillar's tool delivers either.

86
00:05:37.663 --> 00:05:39.612
Three failure patterns recur.

87
00:05:39.612 --> 00:05:42.099
Pillars are administered, not governed.

88
00:05:42.099 --> 00:05:46.106
Evidence is shaped by each tool
rather than by the claim it supports,

89
00:05:46.106 --> 00:05:48.538
so every assessment starts from scratch.

90
00:05:48.538 --> 00:05:52.062
And drift is found at audit
time, months after it opened.

91
00:05:52.062 --> 00:05:54.363
The book answers with five principles.

92
00:05:54.363 --> 00:05:58.065
Identity is the anchor, but
it isn't self-authenticating,

93
00:05:58.065 --> 00:06:01.687
so sessions are re-evaluated
and the plane is defended.

94
00:06:01.687 --> 00:06:04.576
Privileged identity gets
controls of its own.

95
00:06:04.576 --> 00:06:08.788
Evidence must be reproducible,
machine-readable and regenerable.

96
00:06:08.788 --> 00:06:11.693
Governance must enforce, not only observe:

97
00:06:11.693 --> 00:06:16.425
a policy whose only consequence is
next quarter's finding is a report.

98
00:06:16.425 --> 00:06:18.420
And the cloud boundary is modeled,

99
00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:22.625
with each government-cloud feature
gap recorded as a governed finding.

100
00:06:22.625 --> 00:06:26.807
The decision for the CISO, with
identity engineering, this quarter:

101
00:06:26.807 --> 00:06:30.765
time-box privileged access,
including the assessment accounts,

102
00:06:30.765 --> 00:06:34.275
and convert one detective
control into a preventive one.

103
00:06:34.275 --> 00:06:38.583
Microsoft's own guidance is zero
permanently active admin assignments,

104
00:06:38.583 --> 00:06:43.900
other than emergency accounts that are
monitored and tested at least every 90 days.

105
00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:47.175
Now, what the mandates actually require.

106
00:06:47.175 --> 00:06:50.207
Zero Trust programs are
usually argued by citation,

107
00:06:50.207 --> 00:06:54.438
and most of the citations fail when
you check them against the documents.

108
00:06:54.438 --> 00:06:57.101
Four documents get cited
as if they were one.

109
00:06:57.101 --> 00:07:01.046
Executive Order 14028,
from May 2021, is binding,

110
00:07:01.046 --> 00:07:07.825
and required each agency to develop a plan
to implement Zero Trust Architecture.

111
00:07:07.825 --> 00:07:15.400
OMB M-22-09, from January 2022, is binding
and sets specific actions by pillar.

112
00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:20.413
CISA's Zero Trust Maturity
Model, version 2.0, is guidance.

113
00:07:20.413 --> 00:07:25.122
It says it should not be viewed as a strict
set of requirements but as a general

114
00:07:25.122 --> 00:07:29.537
guide, and CISA's page for it is now
marked archived, with no later version.

115
00:07:29.537 --> 00:07:33.702
NIST SP 800-207 is guidance too:

116
00:07:33.702 --> 00:07:37.501
it supplies the tenets and the
architecture the others assume.

117
00:07:37.501 --> 00:07:39.638
The relationship runs one way.

118
00:07:39.638 --> 00:07:44.151
M-22-09 borrows the model's
pillars to organize its actions;

119
00:07:44.151 --> 00:07:47.363
it doesn't adopt the model's
stages as requirements.

120
00:07:47.363 --> 00:07:51.051
That leaves several requirements
that circulate but don't exist.

121
00:07:51.051 --> 00:07:57.438
Neither M-22-09 nor FedRAMP requires a
particular maturity stage, such as Advanced.

122
00:07:57.438 --> 00:08:00.536
No federal document
defines a Zero Trust score,

123
00:08:00.536 --> 00:08:05.938
so a tool whose output certifies maturity
is citing a requirement that isn't there.

124
00:08:05.938 --> 00:08:12.826
M-22-09 doesn't require ScubaGear;
that obligation comes from BOD 25-01.

125
00:08:12.826 --> 00:08:17.350
And inline decryption of all traffic
is neither required nor prohibited.

126
00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:19.250
None of that makes the target vague.

127
00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:23.425
It means the target is a choice
the agency makes and has to defend.

128
00:08:23.425 --> 00:08:27.123
So the decision: set a maturity
target for each pillar,

129
00:08:27.123 --> 00:08:30.400
written down as program
design, not as a mandate.

130
00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:35.080
In the book's responsibility matrix
the Zero Trust program lead decides it,

131
00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:38.779
consulting the CIO, the CISO
and the authorizing official,

132
00:08:38.779 --> 00:08:42.380
and it's needed before the
next assessment is scored.

133
00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:47.202
M-22-09 is the only one of the
four that sets dated actions

134
00:08:47.202 --> 00:08:50.417
for the program itself, and it
isn't limited to the cloud.

135
00:08:50.417 --> 00:08:53.893
It also addresses on-premises
and hybrid systems.

136
00:08:53.893 --> 00:08:56.267
By pillar, the actions are specific.

137
00:08:56.267 --> 00:08:59.021
Identity: centralized identity management,

138
00:08:59.021 --> 00:09:02.965
and phishing-resistant multi-factor
authentication for staff,

139
00:09:02.965 --> 00:09:07.579
contractors and partners, which means
agency systems stop supporting SMS

140
00:09:07.579 --> 00:09:12.118
and voice codes, one-time codes and
push notifications for routine access.

141
00:09:12.118 --> 00:09:17.124
Devices: reliable asset inventories
through CISA's Continuous Diagnostics

142
00:09:17.124 --> 00:09:22.130
and Mitigation program, and endpoint
detection and response deployed widely.

143
00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:27.404
Networks: encrypted DNS, HTTPS for
all web and API traffic, and a plan

144
00:09:27.404 --> 00:09:33.542
for environmental isolation, moving
away from one broad enterprise network.

145
00:09:33.542 --> 00:09:38.925
Applications: dedicated security testing,
a public vulnerability disclosure program,

146
00:09:38.925 --> 00:09:42.244
and at least one internal
FISMA Moderate application

147
00:09:42.244 --> 00:09:45.267
made fully operational
over the public internet.

148
00:09:45.267 --> 00:09:50.080
Data: initial automation of data
categorization, and comprehensive logging.

149
00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:53.243
Its fiscal 2024 deadlines have passed.

150
00:09:53.243 --> 00:09:57.871
OMB hasn't rescinded it: its
memoranda index lists no rescission,

151
00:09:57.871 --> 00:10:00.142
and the memo is still published.

152
00:10:00.142 --> 00:10:02.892
A passed deadline
doesn't retire an action.

153
00:10:02.892 --> 00:10:05.970
Agencies are now measured
against these actions as

154
00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:08.905
an existing obligation
rather than a target date.

155
00:10:08.905 --> 00:10:10.193
There's one adjustment.

156
00:10:10.193 --> 00:10:16.591
The logging action points to M-21-31,
which M-26-14 rescinded on May 22, 2026,

157
00:10:16.591 --> 00:10:24.292
replacing its tiers with a risk-based approach
and a Logging Reference Architecture.

158
00:10:24.292 --> 00:10:29.342
CISA published that architecture on
August 20, 2026, and the memo gives

159
00:10:29.342 --> 00:10:34.305
agencies 90 days from its release
for their first Agency Logging Plan.

160
00:10:34.305 --> 00:10:36.593
That's the decision with the nearest date.

161
00:10:36.593 --> 00:10:41.306
The CISO decides how the Agency Logging
Plan covers Zero Trust telemetry,

162
00:10:41.306 --> 00:10:45.031
including the hosts and accounts
that run the assessments.

163
00:10:45.031 --> 00:10:49.310
It's due 90 days after August 20, 2026.

164
00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:53.935
BOD 25-01 is narrower and more concrete.

165
00:10:53.935 --> 00:10:57.797
CISA issued it on December 17, 2024.

166
00:10:57.797 --> 00:11:00.067
It binds federal civilian agencies,

167
00:11:00.067 --> 00:11:05.060
for every production cloud tenant of a
product with a finalized SCuBA baseline.

168
00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:07.085
It has six required actions.

169
00:11:07.085 --> 00:11:11.092
Inventory the in-scope
tenants, by February 21,

170
00:11:11.092 --> 00:11:14.572
2025, and every year in the first quarter.

171
00:11:14.572 --> 00:11:20.672
Deploy the SCuBA assessment tools and
begin reporting, by April 25, 2025.

172
00:11:20.672 --> 00:11:25.423
Implement the mandatory
policies, by June 20, 2025.

173
00:11:25.423 --> 00:11:28.811
Implement later policy
updates on CISA's timelines.

174
00:11:28.811 --> 00:11:32.645
Baseline every new tenant, and
begin continuous monitoring,

175
00:11:32.645 --> 00:11:35.023
before its authorization to operate.

176
00:11:35.023 --> 00:11:37.648
And explain deviations with each report.

177
00:11:37.648 --> 00:11:41.036
The mandatory list is a
subset of the baselines.

178
00:11:41.036 --> 00:11:47.146
For Microsoft 365, CISA's Required
Configurations page lists 50 policies;

179
00:11:47.146 --> 00:11:51.385
in Entra ID, that's 20 of the
30 in the current baseline.

180
00:11:51.385 --> 00:11:54.613
CISA's guidance limits scope
to software as a service,

181
00:11:54.613 --> 00:11:58.343
so Azure subscriptions are out
even when they share the tenant,

182
00:11:58.343 --> 00:12:02.647
and on-premises Active Directory,
endpoints and networks are untouched.

183
00:12:02.647 --> 00:12:04.885
Google Workspace is now in scope.

184
00:12:04.885 --> 00:12:11.098
Its tenant inventory is due November
2, 2026, and the tools by December 1.

185
00:12:11.098 --> 00:12:14.921
CISA's two pages disagree
on the policy date,

186
00:12:14.921 --> 00:12:18.848
December 1 or December
31, so plan to December 1.

187
00:12:18.848 --> 00:12:23.660
Even a small tenant used by one
component carries all six obligations.

188
00:12:23.660 --> 00:12:24.935
Now count honestly.

189
00:12:24.935 --> 00:12:28.460
Inventory, deployment and
explanations take days.

190
00:12:28.460 --> 00:12:33.260
Actions three to five are architecture,
and they're what the directive exists for.

191
00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:38.231
So measure compliance by the substantive
work: how many mandatory policies hold,

192
00:12:38.231 --> 00:12:41.867
and whether every open deviation
is being fixed on a date or

193
00:12:41.867 --> 00:12:46.022
carried as the authorizing official's
documented risk acceptance.

194
00:12:46.022 --> 00:12:49.478
The CISO owns the inventory
and the reporting route,

195
00:12:49.478 --> 00:12:52.830
and the Workspace inventory
is due November 2.

196
00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:56.155
Reporting is where many agencies stopped,

197
00:12:56.155 --> 00:12:59.980
so it's worth being precise about
what CISA actually receives.

198
00:12:59.980 --> 00:13:04.945
The directive allows continuous integration
with CISA's monitoring, or quarterly

199
00:13:04.945 --> 00:13:09.555
manual reporting, and CISA's implementation
guidance describes four routes.

200
00:13:09.555 --> 00:13:13.376
CISA-hosted ScubaConnect,
where CISA manages reporting

201
00:13:13.376 --> 00:13:16.417
after setup and results
feed the CDM dashboard.

202
00:13:16.417 --> 00:13:21.467
Self-hosted ScubaConnect, where the
agency runs it and keeps local copies.

203
00:13:21.467 --> 00:13:26.257
An agency-hosted integration, through
CLAW or TALON, with automated scans

204
00:13:26.257 --> 00:13:30.968
at least once a quarter, which takes
development work on the agency's side.

205
00:13:30.968 --> 00:13:34.651
Or manual upload to CyberScope,
quarterly or more often,

206
00:13:34.651 --> 00:13:37.293
from the most recent version of the tool.

207
00:13:37.293 --> 00:13:41.620
Whatever the route, the reported
artifact is the tool's own results file,

208
00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:43.893
and agencies are told not to rename it.

209
00:13:43.893 --> 00:13:48.260
A deviation that's operationally required
appears in the output as Omitted,

210
00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:51.657
with its rationale, and the
guidance says omissions must be

211
00:13:51.657 --> 00:13:55.817
approved through the organization's
security risk management process.

212
00:13:55.817 --> 00:14:00.127
CISA also supplies a template for
tracking non-compliant policies,

213
00:14:00.127 --> 00:14:02.093
planned fixes and target dates.

214
00:14:02.093 --> 00:14:05.355
In effect, it's a plan of
action and milestones.

215
00:14:05.355 --> 00:14:08.643
The directive puts the risk
decision in the right place.

216
00:14:08.643 --> 00:14:14.018
Authorizing officials may accept risk for
deviations from the mandatory policies to

217
00:14:14.018 --> 00:14:16.780
meet operational needs,
under agency policy,

218
00:14:16.780 --> 00:14:19.767
and CISA reviews and
resolves what they submit.

219
00:14:19.767 --> 00:14:21.217
Two decisions follow.

220
00:14:21.217 --> 00:14:25.406
The CISO picks the reporting route,
and the assessment operators run it.

221
00:14:25.406 --> 00:14:29.142
Operators never edit results,
and never decide omissions.

222
00:14:29.142 --> 00:14:33.641
And every deviation without an end
date goes to the authorizing official,

223
00:14:33.641 --> 00:14:37.255
because a standing omission is
a standing risk acceptance.

224
00:14:37.255 --> 00:14:39.790
The reporting floor is at least quarterly,

225
00:14:39.790 --> 00:14:43.270
so both are needed before
the next quarterly report.

226
00:14:44.020 --> 00:14:48.619
Agencies run two free, read-only
assessments of their Microsoft cloud,

227
00:14:48.619 --> 00:14:51.632
and often drop one because
it covers identity.

228
00:14:51.632 --> 00:14:53.307
They answer different questions.

229
00:14:53.307 --> 00:14:55.333
ScubaGear is CISA's tool.

230
00:14:55.333 --> 00:15:00.197
It asks a compliance question: does
this Microsoft 365 tenant meet

231
00:15:00.197 --> 00:15:06.033
the federal baselines, including
the BOD 25-01 mandatory policies?

232
00:15:06.033 --> 00:15:10.632
It's the tool the directive names,
and its output is what CISA receives.

233
00:15:10.632 --> 00:15:14.724
Microsoft's Zero Trust Assessment
asks a maturity question:

234
00:15:14.724 --> 00:15:18.980
how close is the Microsoft cloud
environment, across identity,

235
00:15:18.980 --> 00:15:23.071
devices, network and data, to
Microsoft's Zero Trust guidance?

236
00:15:23.071 --> 00:15:30.545
Release 3.1.0, from October
20, 2026, holds 364 tests.

237
00:15:30.545 --> 00:15:34.201
It's a community project
maintained by Microsoft employees,

238
00:15:34.201 --> 00:15:38.295
not an official product, and no
Microsoft support program covers it.

239
00:15:38.295 --> 00:15:41.570
It isn't a BOD 25-01 tool.

240
00:15:41.570 --> 00:15:46.908
They overlap mostly on Entra ID identity,
and now on Defender threat policies.

241
00:15:46.908 --> 00:15:52.390
Only ScubaGear covers Exchange Online,
Teams, SharePoint and Power Platform,

242
00:15:52.390 --> 00:15:56.758
and 22 of the 50 mandatory policies
sit in those four products.

243
00:15:56.758 --> 00:15:59.868
Dropping ScubaGear would
leave mandatory policies

244
00:15:59.868 --> 00:16:03.332
unassessed and stop the
reporting the directive requires.

245
00:16:03.332 --> 00:16:06.534
Dropping the Zero Trust
Assessment leaves devices,

246
00:16:06.534 --> 00:16:10.182
network and data unmeasured
against Microsoft's guidance.

247
00:16:10.182 --> 00:16:15.208
Running both, fully, is still one vendor's
answer about one vendor's platform.

248
00:16:15.208 --> 00:16:18.932
Neither sees another cloud,
the network or the hypervisors.

249
00:16:18.932 --> 00:16:23.720
And neither closes a finding: a score
is a reading, not a treatment plan.

250
00:16:23.720 --> 00:16:28.532
Both outputs are maps of where the tenant
is weak, so they're handled as sensitive.

251
00:16:28.532 --> 00:16:33.224
The decision belongs to the CISO:
approve both tools, with pinned versions,

252
00:16:33.224 --> 00:16:37.236
for production tenants, and
require their output to stay inside

253
00:16:37.236 --> 00:16:41.020
the authorization boundary,
before the next production run.

254
00:16:41.020 --> 00:16:46.050
Expect the score to move when a version
changes, even when the tenant didn't.

255
00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:52.362
Everything so far stops at the edge of
Microsoft 365 and Google Workspace.

256
00:16:52.362 --> 00:16:57.046
Nothing on BOD 25-01's list
covers infrastructure accounts,

257
00:16:57.046 --> 00:17:00.012
the agency network or
the virtualized estate.

258
00:17:00.012 --> 00:17:03.697
So the whole-agency argument
rests on the Zero Trust documents,

259
00:17:03.697 --> 00:17:10.287
and on the SP 800-53 controls every
system security plan already asserts.

260
00:17:10.287 --> 00:17:13.900
Three things get reconciled before
those estates are assessed.

261
00:17:13.900 --> 00:17:20.194
One inventory: control CM-8 forbids
duplicate accounting of components, and BOD

262
00:17:20.194 --> 00:17:24.485
23-01 requires automated
asset discovery every 7 days,

263
00:17:24.485 --> 00:17:27.250
including cloud virtual machines.

264
00:17:27.250 --> 00:17:28.831
One naming authority,

265
00:17:28.831 --> 00:17:33.575
because each cloud provider and
VMware ships its own private DNS.

266
00:17:33.575 --> 00:17:38.616
And no implicit trust from any boundary:
a site-to-site VPN, a default virtual

267
00:17:38.616 --> 00:17:43.900
network and a flat virtualized segment
are architecturally the same mistake.

268
00:17:43.900 --> 00:17:48.519
The hypervisor is a distinct threat class,
so its management plane is assessed

269
00:17:48.519 --> 00:17:52.925
as its own surface, not inferred from
the posture of the guests running on it.

270
00:17:52.925 --> 00:17:57.113
Then the providers' own tools, each
of which describes its own limits.

271
00:17:57.113 --> 00:18:00.064
AWS says its NIST standard
supports a subset

272
00:18:00.064 --> 00:18:02.937
of requirements through automated checks.

273
00:18:02.937 --> 00:18:08.638
Microsoft says Azure Policy compliance is
only a partial view of overall compliance.

274
00:18:08.638 --> 00:18:11.886
Broadcom calls its VMware
hardening material general

275
00:18:11.886 --> 00:18:15.488
guidance whose assumptions may
not hold in your environment.

276
00:18:15.488 --> 00:18:18.473
And FedRAMP certifies the
cloud service offering,

277
00:18:18.473 --> 00:18:21.530
while each agency still
authorizes its own systems,

278
00:18:21.530 --> 00:18:25.937
including the configuration it selected
and the integrations it enabled.

279
00:18:25.937 --> 00:18:28.112
The decision is the program lead's:

280
00:18:28.112 --> 00:18:32.381
admit each native tool's findings
as evidence for its own platform,

281
00:18:32.381 --> 00:18:36.651
under the CISO's evidence standard,
and never as the agency's score.

282
00:18:36.651 --> 00:18:40.380
It's needed before any
whole-agency figure is reported.

283
00:18:41.130 --> 00:18:41.980
Who owns it?

284
00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:45.567
Zero Trust programs rarely
fail for lack of owners.

285
00:18:45.567 --> 00:18:48.505
They fail because every
owner is a pillar owner.

286
00:18:48.505 --> 00:18:53.373
Nobody owns the question this book keeps
asking: what is the agency's posture across

287
00:18:53.373 --> 00:18:57.817
all five pillars, and what does the number
on the cover page actually measure?

288
00:18:57.817 --> 00:19:00.393
The book's model separates
deciding from doing.

289
00:19:00.393 --> 00:19:06.004
The CIO and executive sponsor set strategy,
scope and funding, and break ties.

290
00:19:06.004 --> 00:19:11.104
Three CIO decisions matter here: which
estates the whole-agency score covers,

291
00:19:11.104 --> 00:19:16.044
stated in writing so it can't quietly
shrink to the estates that assess well;

292
00:19:16.044 --> 00:19:22.099
the sequence, since closing BOD 25-01
findings competes for the same engineers

293
00:19:22.099 --> 00:19:26.880
as every other deadline; and which team
owns a control that spans pillars.

294
00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:31.421
The Zero Trust program lead owns the
posture: the definition of the score,

295
00:19:31.421 --> 00:19:36.568
cross-pillar correlation, the assessment
calendar and the prioritized worklist.

296
00:19:36.568 --> 00:19:40.253
Many agencies will give this
role to the implementation

297
00:19:40.253 --> 00:19:44.567
lead M-22-09 required them
to designate within 30 days.

298
00:19:44.567 --> 00:19:47.608
The lead decides priority
but doesn't accept risk,

299
00:19:47.608 --> 00:19:52.429
and shouldn't also own the agency's
relationship with its largest cloud vendor.

300
00:19:52.429 --> 00:19:54.868
That independence is
the point of the role.

301
00:19:54.868 --> 00:19:58.612
The CISO sets the control requirements
and the evidence standard,

302
00:19:58.612 --> 00:20:01.555
and approves the tools and
the accounts that run them.

303
00:20:01.555 --> 00:20:04.630
The authorizing official
accepts residual risk,

304
00:20:04.630 --> 00:20:09.430
approves each plan of action and
milestones, and sets assessor independence.

305
00:20:09.430 --> 00:20:12.543
Most agencies have several,
so the lead aggregates,

306
00:20:12.543 --> 00:20:16.693
and each official accepts risk only
for the systems they authorize.

307
00:20:16.693 --> 00:20:20.423
The decision is the CIO's, and
the book puts it in this quarter:

308
00:20:20.423 --> 00:20:23.698
name an agency-level owner
of the Zero Trust posture,

309
00:20:23.698 --> 00:20:29.480
and a cross-pillar correlation owner, separate
from any cloud provider relationship.

310
00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:33.439
Pillar by pillar, the book
maps CISA's five pillars

311
00:20:33.439 --> 00:20:36.418
to the teams that own
them after modernization.

312
00:20:36.418 --> 00:20:39.117
Identity engineering owns identity.

313
00:20:39.117 --> 00:20:41.618
Endpoint engineering owns devices.

314
00:20:41.618 --> 00:20:45.918
Network engineering, with security
architecture, owns networks.

315
00:20:45.918 --> 00:20:50.868
Application owners, with the platform
teams, own applications and workloads.

316
00:20:50.868 --> 00:20:53.781
And the data owners and
the data office own data,

317
00:20:53.781 --> 00:20:56.993
the one pillar without a
workbook book of its own yet.

318
00:20:56.993 --> 00:20:58.629
More owners sit across them.

319
00:20:58.629 --> 00:21:02.311
The naming and identity
plane belongs to identity

320
00:21:02.311 --> 00:21:06.255
engineering and the DNS and
DDI team, governed as Tier 0.

321
00:21:06.255 --> 00:21:09.178
Each other cloud and the
virtualization estate

322
00:21:09.178 --> 00:21:12.393
belongs to its platform team,
reconciled to the plane.

323
00:21:12.393 --> 00:21:16.317
Security operations owns
visibility and analytics.

324
00:21:16.317 --> 00:21:19.706
Pillar owners own automation,
under gated change.

325
00:21:19.706 --> 00:21:24.197
And governance, the across-pillars part
no pillar owner can deliver alone,

326
00:21:24.197 --> 00:21:29.130
sits with the program lead, together with
the CISO and the authorizing official.

327
00:21:29.130 --> 00:21:31.692
Pillar owners decide how to fix a finding.

328
00:21:31.692 --> 00:21:34.005
They don't decide that
it doesn't need fixing.

329
00:21:34.005 --> 00:21:36.630
Three calls often land on the wrong desk.

330
00:21:36.630 --> 00:21:40.809
First, an omission or incorrect-result
marking with an expiration

331
00:21:40.809 --> 00:21:45.292
date is a CISO decision, recorded in
the versioned configuration file.

332
00:21:45.292 --> 00:21:50.047
Second, one with no end date is a
standing risk acceptance, and goes to the

333
00:21:50.047 --> 00:21:56.092
authorizing official, which matches BOD
25-01's assignment of deviation risk.

334
00:21:56.092 --> 00:22:00.583
Third, drift, a control that passed
every interval and failed the last one,

335
00:22:00.583 --> 00:22:05.673
goes to the authorizing official too, because
it's a change to an authorized system,

336
00:22:05.673 --> 00:22:08.817
and the official decides
whether it's significant.

337
00:22:08.817 --> 00:22:11.315
The decision for the CISO, this quarter:

338
00:22:11.315 --> 00:22:16.233
move every exception out of documents
and into the configuration file and the

339
00:22:16.233 --> 00:22:18.200
repository, with an expiration,

340
00:22:18.200 --> 00:22:22.100
and route each standing one
to the authorizing official.

341
00:22:22.850 --> 00:22:23.875
How do you get there?

342
00:22:23.875 --> 00:22:26.200
First, close before you assess.

343
00:22:26.200 --> 00:22:29.959
Agencies met BOD 25-01's early dates:

344
00:22:29.959 --> 00:22:34.375
tenants inventoried, ScubaGear
deployed and reporting.

345
00:22:34.375 --> 00:22:36.375
Many now treat that as the answer.

346
00:22:36.375 --> 00:22:39.710
But ScubaGear measures
deviation from a baseline;

347
00:22:39.710 --> 00:22:43.838
it doesn't close any, and neither
does the Zero Trust Assessment.

348
00:22:43.838 --> 00:22:48.463
A thermometer that reports a fever every
day for a year isn't a treatment plan.

349
00:22:48.463 --> 00:22:52.685
Without an architecture to absorb
the fix, remediation collapses

350
00:22:52.685 --> 00:22:56.676
into re-running the scan and filing
another plan-of-action line.

351
00:22:56.676 --> 00:23:00.749
So the program's six-phase sequence
puts the assessors at step five,

352
00:23:00.749 --> 00:23:05.787
as confirmation rather than discovery:
understand the gaps and their root causes,

353
00:23:05.787 --> 00:23:10.231
design the closure pattern, implement
it, govern it with owners and plans,

354
00:23:10.231 --> 00:23:14.750
then validate, and then the authorizing
official decides on the evidence.

355
00:23:14.750 --> 00:23:18.398
That's the Risk Management
Framework's own order:

356
00:23:18.398 --> 00:23:21.225
implement, assess, authorize, monitor.

357
00:23:21.225 --> 00:23:24.981
BOD 25-01 says the same for new tenants:

358
00:23:24.981 --> 00:23:28.738
baseline first, then the
authorization to operate.

359
00:23:28.738 --> 00:23:32.880
Before validating, build the layers
a finding depends on: naming,

360
00:23:32.880 --> 00:23:36.787
addressing and certificates;
device and workload identity;

361
00:23:36.787 --> 00:23:41.476
a phased retirement of legacy
authentication; and governed exceptions.

362
00:23:41.476 --> 00:23:43.988
Not every finding waits for architecture.

363
00:23:43.988 --> 00:23:49.049
Route each one to exactly one response:
a configuration change that closes now;

364
00:23:49.049 --> 00:23:52.319
architectural work with an
owner and a target date;

365
00:23:52.319 --> 00:23:57.613
or a risk acceptance by the authorizing
official, marked Omitted with a rationale.

366
00:23:57.613 --> 00:24:00.331
A finding with no response recorded is the

367
00:24:00.331 --> 00:24:03.050
only state the program
treats as a failure.

368
00:24:03.050 --> 00:24:08.998
This is the CIO's sequence decision:
close the BOD 25-01 findings before the

369
00:24:08.998 --> 00:24:14.450
maturity assessment, and staff that order
against the agency's other deadlines.

370
00:24:14.450 --> 00:24:18.010
It's needed before the maturity
assessment is scheduled.

371
00:24:18.760 --> 00:24:19.948
Then the evidence.

372
00:24:19.948 --> 00:24:22.850
The two tools use different
words for the same outcomes,

373
00:24:22.850 --> 00:24:25.373
and some of their outcomes
aren't findings at all.

374
00:24:25.373 --> 00:24:29.298
Only four results are findings:
ScubaGear's Fail and Warning,

375
00:24:29.298 --> 00:24:32.909
and the Zero Trust Assessment's
Failed and Investigate.

376
00:24:32.909 --> 00:24:36.267
A not-applicable result
is usually a manual check

377
00:24:36.267 --> 00:24:39.460
for the pillar owner to
verify by hand, not a pass.

378
00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:43.635
An error is absence of evidence:
fix the run and re-run.

379
00:24:43.635 --> 00:24:46.798
And a skipped check isn't
a gap and isn't a pass.

380
00:24:46.798 --> 00:24:50.684
The tool's own guide says missing
prerequisites produce skipped checks,

381
00:24:50.684 --> 00:24:53.534
not evidence that the tenant
meets a recommendation.

382
00:24:53.534 --> 00:24:55.331
Often the reason is a license,

383
00:24:55.331 --> 00:24:59.597
or a feature that isn't available or
authorized in a government cloud.

384
00:24:59.597 --> 00:25:02.334
Scoring it as a failure
overstates the gap;

385
00:25:02.334 --> 00:25:05.148
scoring it as a pass
overstates the posture.

386
00:25:05.148 --> 00:25:07.674
So it's recorded as absence of evidence,

387
00:25:07.674 --> 00:25:10.911
with the reason, and it
becomes a boundary finding.

388
00:25:10.911 --> 00:25:16.580
The program then scores the real findings
with a documented method: severity

389
00:25:16.580 --> 00:25:22.072
from 0 to 100, priority as severity over
effort, and buckets from P1 down to P4.

390
00:25:22.072 --> 00:25:24.522
Quick wins go into the next change window.

391
00:25:24.522 --> 00:25:28.244
The method is the program's,
not a federal standard,

392
00:25:28.244 --> 00:25:31.422
and a P1 is a priority,
not a risk acceptance.

393
00:25:31.422 --> 00:25:35.061
Last, reconcile at the
control, not tool to tool.

394
00:25:35.061 --> 00:25:40.007
A ScubaGear failure and a Zero Trust
Assessment failure on the same setting and the

395
00:25:40.007 --> 00:25:46.245
same SP 800-53 control are one
finding with two pieces of evidence,

396
00:25:46.245 --> 00:25:47.822
routed to one pillar owner.

397
00:25:47.822 --> 00:25:51.854
Underneath all of this sits
the CISO's evidence standard:

398
00:25:51.854 --> 00:25:57.147
a pinned tool version, a recorded run
time, an unaltered output file and a hash.

399
00:25:57.147 --> 00:26:00.960
It's needed before any result
is scored or reported.

400
00:26:01.710 --> 00:26:06.148
Now turn the worklist into a program
the authorizing official can see.

401
00:26:06.148 --> 00:26:10.367
Every finding that isn't fixed in the
next change window and isn't covered

402
00:26:10.367 --> 00:26:14.447
by an approved exception becomes a
plan of action and milestones item.

403
00:26:14.447 --> 00:26:20.277
SP 800-53 requires one for each
system, updated from assessments

404
00:26:20.277 --> 00:26:24.209
and continuous monitoring, and the
authorizing official approves it.

405
00:26:24.209 --> 00:26:28.269
Each item names the pillar owner,
the control it bears on, the evidence

406
00:26:28.269 --> 00:26:32.685
that will show it closed, and the
re-assessment that will test that evidence.

407
00:26:32.685 --> 00:26:36.413
Behind those items, the program
wraps each tool's unaltered

408
00:26:36.413 --> 00:26:39.760
output with its provenance
and seals it into a bundle.

409
00:26:39.760 --> 00:26:43.138
Because the same inputs produce
a byte-identical bundle,

410
00:26:43.138 --> 00:26:46.588
an assessor can rebuild it
months later and walk back from

411
00:26:46.588 --> 00:26:49.822
a finding to the raw result
without re-running the tool.

412
00:26:49.822 --> 00:26:53.435
That's the difference between an
archived opinion and evidence.

413
00:26:53.435 --> 00:26:58.012
Re-assessment runs on a calendar the
program lead owns, and after each

414
00:26:58.012 --> 00:27:02.910
remediation wave, so a finding is closed
by evidence, not by a ticket status.

415
00:27:02.910 --> 00:27:08.373
BOD 25-01's reporting routes set the
floor for ScubaGear at quarterly.

416
00:27:08.373 --> 00:27:12.080
A Zero Trust Assessment score
is compared across runs only

417
00:27:12.080 --> 00:27:15.636
on the same tool version, or
after a review of what changed.

418
00:27:15.636 --> 00:27:20.585
And drift goes to the authorizing
official, not only to security operations.

419
00:27:20.585 --> 00:27:23.465
A control that passed
every interval and failed

420
00:27:23.465 --> 00:27:26.273
the last one is a change
to an authorized system.

421
00:27:26.273 --> 00:27:29.748
The book calls treating that
drift as something the official

422
00:27:29.748 --> 00:27:32.747
sees the cheapest adoption
step in its final chapter.

423
00:27:32.747 --> 00:27:35.801
So the decision sits with
the authorizing official:

424
00:27:35.801 --> 00:27:38.500
receive interval-to-interval
drift findings,

425
00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:41.695
prepared by the program lead
and security operations,

426
00:27:41.695 --> 00:27:46.098
every assessment interval, and decide
which ones are significant changes.

427
00:27:46.098 --> 00:27:47.870
Stand it up this quarter.

428
00:27:48.620 --> 00:27:51.320
So, the decisions, with their owners.

429
00:27:51.320 --> 00:27:53.082
None of them is a procurement.

430
00:27:53.082 --> 00:27:56.731
The CIO names an agency-level
owner of the Zero Trust posture,

431
00:27:56.731 --> 00:28:01.912
and a cross-pillar correlation owner, separate
from any cloud provider relationship,

432
00:28:01.912 --> 00:28:05.196
and puts the scope of the
whole-agency score in writing.

433
00:28:05.196 --> 00:28:09.510
The identity-plane owner reconciles
the network, every cloud and the

434
00:28:09.510 --> 00:28:14.582
virtualization estate to one authoritative
record before any of them is scored.

435
00:28:14.582 --> 00:28:19.074
The program lead finds out, pillar by
pillar, which evidence can be re-run

436
00:28:19.074 --> 00:28:23.719
without the operator's help, because
every no marks the adoption boundary.

437
00:28:23.719 --> 00:28:28.970
The CISO moves every exception into the
configuration file, with an expiration;

438
00:28:28.970 --> 00:28:33.436
covers the assessment hosts and
accounts in the Agency Logging Plan,

439
00:28:33.436 --> 00:28:35.160
due 90 days after August 20;

440
00:28:35.160 --> 00:28:39.861
brings any Google Workspace tenant
into the inventory by November 2; and,

441
00:28:39.861 --> 00:28:43.622
with identity engineering,
time-boxes privileged access

442
00:28:43.622 --> 00:28:47.383
and converts one detective
control into a preventive one.

443
00:28:47.383 --> 00:28:51.435
The program lead and security
operations stand up drift detection,

444
00:28:51.435 --> 00:28:54.619
and the authorizing official
receives its findings.

445
00:28:54.619 --> 00:28:59.075
And the system owner and ISSO record
each government-cloud feature

446
00:28:59.075 --> 00:29:03.132
gap as a governed finding, rather
than an unstated assumption.

447
00:29:03.132 --> 00:29:05.807
Each is a structural step that compounds.

448
00:29:05.807 --> 00:29:07.483
Unify first, assess second,

449
00:29:07.483 --> 00:29:11.445
and let named people make the
decisions the evidence supports.

450
00:29:11.445 --> 00:29:16.070
No tool, and no number of tools,
certifies Zero Trust maturity.

451
